  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 217 PM EDT Tue Aug 25 2026\n\nCurrent-Tonight...Another warm & humid day with max temps in the L-\nM90s this afternoon and peak heat indices above 100F, still \nremaining below Heat Advisory criteria. Residents and visitors are \nstrongly encouraged to practice heat safety to avoid developing heat \nillness. Weak mid-level troughing continues across north FL and will \nenhance diurnal shower/lightning storms this afternoon and evening. \nHighest chances (50-70%) from Osceola-Brevard County northward where \nmoisture is deepest, and generally 20-40% PoPs southward. The ECSB \nwill develop and push inland this afternoon with strongest boundary \ncollisions across the interior to include Volusia County. Storm \nmotion is weak/erratic and some storms may push back to the \nVolusia/Brevard coast this evening. Primary storm impacts remain \nlocal strong wind gusts (45-55 mph) in a few storms, frequent \nlightning strikes, and torrential downpours leading to quick 1-3\" \nrainfall totals. Localized amounts of 4-5\" may be possible and the\nWeather Prediction Center has outlined central Brevard - central\nOsceola County northward for a Marginal Risk of Excessive Rainfall\nthis afternoon/evening. Activity will wind down through mid/late  \nevening and cloud-cover will thin thru the overnight. Min \ntemperatures fall into the M70s with conditions humid. \n\nWed-Wed Night...Surface ridge axis will advance northward across \ncentral FL. Light morning southerly winds will \"back\" onshore in the \nafternoon with sea breeze formation and push inland. ISOLD-SCT \nshowers storms with the ECSB near the coast - earlier development \nand quicker movement inland south; the WCSB will promote similar \ninitial coverage across our western coverage warning area in the \nafternoon. A late day/evening sea breeze collision likely from \nnear Lake George thru Seminole County, Greater Orlando Metro and \nsouth across the Kissimmee River. PoPs 60-70% nearly areawide. \n\nHigh temps continue in the L-M90s with peak heat indices north of \n100F. Overnight lows consistent in the M70s with conditions muggy.\n\nPrevious Extended Forecast Discussion Slightly Modified.\n\nThu-Fri...Sea-breeze-driven showers and storms are likely each day \nas moisture values (PWATs 2\"+) return to normal to slightly above. A \nfew storms will likely develop near the coast in the early afternoon \nbefore a collision over the interior sparks the greatest coverage \nand intensity late in the afternoon or by early evening. Weak \noffshore flow in the mid-levels may drive some lingering storms back \ntoward the east coast in the evening. Each day, rain and storm \nchances range from 50-70%. Our general summertime thunderstorm \nhazards are expected, with frequent lightning, wind gusts from 40 to \n45 mph, and localized flooding the main threats.\n\nSeasonably hot temperatures and humidity levels will continue.\n\nWeekend-Early Next Week...Rather typical late summer weather is \nexpected to persist as August wraps up across east central Florida. \nLight onshore flow tries to become established this weekend as high \npressure becomes situated just to our northeast. With no \ninterruption to the moist airmass overhead, expect scattered to \nnumerous afternoon and evening storms, with the focus likely \nbecoming more over the interior with slightly less coverage near the \ncoast. Hot and muggy each afternoon before storms begin to form.",
